#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""hazlint -- refuse a payload that reads a register in the load delay slot.

Why this scans a binary, and not a `.s` file
--------------------------------------------
The failure this exists to stop has happened once, on this silicon, and it is
on the record: upstream's `P9-12`, 2026-08-21 (`upstream/PROGRESS.md:6270`,
`upstream/RUNBOOK.md:4003`, `upstream/BENCH-LOG.md:4460` `T-89`/`T-90`).

A payload jumped, ran, and printed exactly 16 bytes per iteration for ten
minutes.  `andi t2,t2,0x60` sat in the load delay slot of `lbu t2,0(t1)` and
therefore masked the PREVIOUS line-status reading, which is permanently
non-zero after the first character.  The wait loop never waited, 41 bytes went
out back to back, the 16550's 16-deep transmit FIFO took 16 and dropped 25 --
the nonce among them.

**The delay slot was filled.  It was filled with the wrong instruction.**

That is the whole design argument for this tool:

  * A checker that asks "is the delay slot empty" passes that payload.
  * A checker that reads `.s` files never sees that payload at all, because it
    came out of an encoder in `tools/mkramboot.py`, not out of an assembler.
  * `mkramboot.py` DID simulate every build, and it certified this one.  It
    models a core with load interlocks.  This one has none.  A model kinder
    than the device certifies exactly the bugs the device will reject.

So the check is: **for every load, does an instruction that can execute next
read the register that was loaded.**

What it checks
--------------
Main check (default).  A linear scan over 4-byte aligned big-endian words.

  * A **load** is a MIPS-I load that writes a general register --
    `lb lbu lh lhu lw lwl lwr` -- whose `rt` is not `$zero`.
  * A **violation** is any instruction that can be the next one executed and
    that reads that `rt` as a general-register source.
  * `lwl`/`lwr` immediately followed by the matching `lwr`/`lwl` on the same
    `rt` is exempt.  MIPS-I defines that pair as the unaligned-access idiom and
    exempts it by name.  (Whether this Lexra core implements those four
    instructions at all is `C-7`/`F51`, still open -- the `--isa` mode is what
    reports them.)
  * If the load sits in a **delay slot**, the next instruction executed is the
    branch target, not the next word.  This tool computes the target for
    `j`/`jal`/`b`/`bal`, for the REGIMM branches including the four
    branch-likely forms, and for the coprocessor branches, and it checks BOTH
    legs of a conditional.  For `jr`/`jalr`, for a target outside the span, and
    for an unlinked object's placeholder target, it reports `unresolved` rather
    than silently checking the wrong word.  Measured on `stage2.bin`: **0 loads
    sit in any delay slot**, so this path does not touch the population control
    -- it exists for hand-written asm.
  * **Nothing is read across a span boundary.**  Two `PT_LOAD`s, or two sections
    of a `.o`, are not one run of instructions; the last word of one is not the
    predecessor of the first word of the next.  In a relocatable object every
    section reports `sh_addr == 0`, so each gets a synthetic base, the report
    says the addresses are synthetic, and any absolute target computed from one
    is reported unresolved instead of followed.

`unresolved` and `notes` are different claims
---------------------------------------------
**unresolved**: the successor could not be determined.  An unchecked successor
is an unchecked load, so this exits 1.

**note**: the successor WAS determined and checked; what this file cannot rule
out is a *second* one.  The only case today is word 0 of a section, whose
predecessor is whatever the linker eventually places before it.  Stated on every
run, and it does not fail the gate -- which is why the gate is pointed at the
**linked** image and not at a `.o`.

Subsidiary check, `--isa`.  ISA-level conformance: every instruction outside
what this core is known to implement, with the code/data boundary given by the
caller and the hex plus ASCII of every hit printed beside it.  Retained from
this item's fourth revision; the fifth moved the weight to the main check.

What it cannot do, stated so a clean result is not read as more than it is
------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * A linear scan decodes data as code.  It is a **superset** of the
    instruction stream: it can count data as code but it cannot miss an
    instruction.  So a **zero is rigorous and a non-zero needs adjudication**,
    which is why every hit prints its word and its ASCII.
  * It models the load-use hazard only.  `mflo`/`mfhi` after `mult`/`div`, and
    the `mtc0` hazards, are `C-9`/`F47` and the rules for them are NOT
    established on this core.  `--survey` counts those shapes and refuses to
    call them violations, because a verdict without a rule is a guess wearing a
    number.
  * It says nothing about whether an instruction is REACHABLE.  A violation in
    dead code is still reported; that is the safe direction.
  * It cannot tell you the core's hazard rule.  It assumes the rule that
    `stage2.bin` demonstrates the vendor's own compiler obeying, and `R1`
    settles it on silicon.

The controls, and why a clean run means something
-------------------------------------------------
Every invocation runs the controls first and **refuses to report if any of them
fails**.  `--self-test` runs them and nothing else.

  K1  decoder cases.  Hand-encoded (load, next) pairs with an expected verdict:
      cases that MUST fire and near-misses that MUST NOT.
  K2  negative control: upstream's own broken payload, the 148-byte `P9-12` v1.
      It is not a hand-written fixture -- it is the image that failed on this
      physical device.  Expected: **2 violations**, at file offsets `0x1c` and
      `0x2c`.
  K3  differential positive control: the same payload with the fix, 156 bytes.
      Expected: 2 loads, 2 `nop`s, **0 violations**.  K2 and K3 differ by two
      `nop`s and nothing else, and this tool asserts that too -- so the pair is
      a single-variable experiment on the checker itself.
  K4  **population control**: `stage2.bin`, the loader's own second stage.
      Expected: **1,474 loads / 646 followed by `nop` / 0 violations.**
      This is the control that answers the question a zero cannot:
      **a tool that is not looking reports 0 loads, not 0 violations.**
      If `stage2.bin` cannot be found, this tool REFUSES to certify anything:
      `--no-population-control` lets you look at a file anyway, but the exit
      code is then 2 whatever the file says, so no build gate can pass on it.
  K5  endianness and alignment: the K2 fixture read little-endian, and read
      from a 2-byte offset, must NOT reproduce K2's answer.  A scanner that
      ignored either would still be a scanner -- it would just be wrong.
  K7  delay-slot successors.  Whole-span cases: an unconditional branch, a
      REGIMM branch-likely, a coprocessor branch, a register jump, and an
      unlinked `jal`.  Each puts something clean on the leg that is not taken
      and a register read on the leg that is, so a checker that follows the
      wrong leg reports 0.
  K8  span boundary: a load at the end of one span and a read of it at the start
      of the next must NOT be a violation, and must be reported.
  K6  ISA conformance (`--isa` and `--self-test`): `stage2.bin`'s code region
      `[0x80400000, 0x8040A000)` must give **exactly 18** hits -- `movz` x 12,
      `movn` x 6 -- at the addresses `docs/loader-command-semantics.md` §9
      lists one by one, and the data region must reproduce the two false
      positives that document names by address.

Using it as a gate, which is the half that matters tomorrow
-----------------------------------------------------------
`P9-12`'s own conclusion was that catching it once is layer one and layer two
is what stops the next payload.  So the exit code is the product:

    0  clean, and every control held
    1  at least one violation, or a successor that could not be resolved
    2  refused: a control failed, the population control did not run, or the
       scan found no loads at all
    3  usage error

`rlxprobe`'s build must run this on the linked payload and stop on anything but
0.  Not a lint whose output someone reads: a gate that fails the build.

Version 1.1, 2026-08-24
-----------------------
An adversarial review of 1.0 -- four independent lenses, every claim put to a
separate agent whose job was to refute it -- returned **17 confirmed defects out
of 24 claimed**.  The two that mattered most:

  * The `lwl`/`lwr` pair exemption skipped the whole successor rather than the
    `rt` merge alone, so it swallowed the successor's **base** register too --
    and because `reads()` did not model the merge either, the exemption had only
    ever suppressed true positives.  Both halves are fixed together.
  * **K4 built its words with a private helper**, so the population control
    exercised a code path no scanned file ever took.  It could not have caught
    any defect in span construction, and it looked exactly like it could.  It
    now goes through `raw_span()`, which is what `--raw` builds for a user file.

The rest were span handling (six), scan coverage (two), decoder gaps (three) and
control gaps (four).  `tools/test-hazlint.sh` grew M7-M9, U1 and U2 so that each
fix has a mutation that fails without it -- the review's finding 6 was that the
patched tool and the shipped tool were indistinguishable to the old suite.

Usage
    tools/hazlint FILE [--base ADDR] [--range LO:HI] [--raw] [--section NAME]
                       [--survey] [--max-report N]
                       [--stage2 PATH] [--no-population-control]
                       [--allow-zero-loads]
    tools/hazlint --isa FILE [--code-range LO:HI] [--loose] [--base ADDR] ...
    tools/hazlint --self-test
"""

import hashlib
import os
import struct
import sys

VERSION = "1.1"

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The instruction model
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

REGS = ('zero', 'at', 'v0', 'v1', 'a0', 'a1', 'a2', 'a3',
        't0', 't1', 't2', 't3', 't4', 't5', 't6', 't7',
        's0', 's1', 's2', 's3', 's4', 's5', 's6', 's7',
        't8', 't9', 'k0', 'k1', 'gp', 'sp', 's8', 'ra')

# A load, here, is a MIPS-I load that writes a general register.
#
# `ll` (0x30), `lwc1` (0x31) and `ldc1` (0x35) are deliberately NOT on this
# list.  This is a Lexra-family MIPS-I core: `ll` is MIPS-II and the two
# coprocessor loads need an FPU that is not there.  The decision is checkable
# rather than a matter of taste -- the one word in `stage2.bin` that decodes as
# `ll` is at `0x8040AB14` and is `c0a80001`, which is `192.168.0.1`, and the
# one that decodes as `lwr` is at `0x8040D760` in the same table region.  Both
# are named as data in `docs/loader-command-semantics.md` §9, so both fall out
# of the population control for a stated reason rather than by being subtracted
# afterwards to make a number come out.
LOADS = {0x20: 'lb', 0x21: 'lh', 0x22: 'lwl', 0x23: 'lw',
         0x24: 'lbu', 0x25: 'lhu', 0x26: 'lwr'}
MERGING = (0x22, 0x26)          # lwl / lwr: rt is a source as well as a target


def rs_of(w):
    return (w >> 21) & 0x1F


def rt_of(w):
    return (w >> 16) & 0x1F


def rd_of(w):
    return (w >> 11) & 0x1F


def sa_of(w):
    return (w >> 6) & 0x1F


def simm(w):
    v = w & 0xFFFF
    return v - 0x10000 if v & 0x8000 else v


def load_of(w):
    """(mnemonic, rt) if w is a load that writes a general register, else None."""
    op = w >> 26
    if op in LOADS and rt_of(w) != 0:
        return (LOADS[op], rt_of(w))
    return None


def reads(w):
    """The set of general registers this instruction reads.

    Conservative in the direction of reporting: an encoding this table does not
    recognise is assumed to read `rs`, `rt` and `rd`.  A checker that guessed
    the other way would be a model kinder than the device, which is the exact
    thing `P9-12` was.
    """
    op = w >> 26
    rs, rt, rd, f = rs_of(w), rt_of(w), rd_of(w), w & 0x3F

    if op == 0x00:                                  # SPECIAL
        if f in (0x00, 0x02, 0x03):                 # sll srl sra
            return {rt}
        if f in (0x04, 0x06, 0x07):                 # sllv srlv srav
            return {rs, rt}
        if f in (0x08, 0x09):                       # jr jalr
            return {rs}
        if f in (0x0C, 0x0D):                       # syscall break
            return set()
        if f == 0x0F:                               # sync (MIPS-II)
            return set()
        if f in (0x0A, 0x0B):                       # movz movn (MIPS-IV)
            # rd is architecturally preserved rather than read, but a checker
            # that assumed so would be assuming.  Counted as read.
            return {rs, rt, rd}
        if f in (0x10, 0x12):                       # mfhi mflo
            return set()
        if f in (0x11, 0x13):                       # mthi mtlo
            return {rs}
        if f in (0x18, 0x19, 0x1A, 0x1B):           # mult multu div divu
            return {rs, rt}
        if 0x20 <= f <= 0x2B:                       # add .. sltu
            return {rs, rt}
        if 0x30 <= f <= 0x36:                       # traps (MIPS-II)
            return {rs, rt}
        return {rs, rt, rd}                         # unknown SPECIAL

    if op == 0x01:                                  # REGIMM: bltz bgez ...
        return {rs}
    if op in (0x02, 0x03):                          # j jal
        return set()
    if op in (0x04, 0x05, 0x14, 0x15):              # beq bne beql bnel
        return {rs, rt}
    if op in (0x06, 0x07, 0x16, 0x17):              # blez bgtz blezl bgtzl
        return {rs}
    if 0x08 <= op <= 0x0E:                          # addi .. xori
        return {rs}
    if op == 0x0F:                                  # lui
        return set()
    if op in (0x10, 0x11, 0x12):                    # COP0 COP1 COP2
        # MT/CT move a general register INTO the coprocessor, so they read rt;
        # MF/CF write rt.  Any other rs -- and this loader has four words at
        # 0x80400C40..0x80400C7C with rs = 3, which no source held here names --
        # is treated as reading rt, which is the reporting direction.
        return set() if rs in (0x00, 0x01, 0x02, 0x08) else {rt}
    if op == 0x13:                                  # COP1X (MIPS-IV)
        return {rs, rt}
    if op in MERGING:
        # `lwl`/`lwr` merge into rt, so **rt is a source as well as the
        # target**.  This file's own MERGING comment said so and this function
        # did not, which made the pair exemption in scan() suppress nothing at
        # all -- it could only ever fire on a case that was already invisible.
        # Found by the adversarial review, 2026-08-24.
        return {rs, rt}
    if op in LOADS or op in (0x30, 0x31, 0x33, 0x35, 0x2F):
        return {rs}                                 # loads, ll, pref, cache
    if op in (0x28, 0x29, 0x2A, 0x2B, 0x2E, 0x38):  # sb sh swl sw swr sc
        return {rs, rt}
    if op in (0x39, 0x3D):                          # swc1 sdc1
        return {rs}
    if op in (0x1C, 0x1F):                          # SPECIAL2 SPECIAL3
        return {rs, rt}
    if op == 0x1D:                                  # jalx
        return set()
    return {rs, rt}                                 # unknown primary opcode


def control_flow(w, addr):
    """(kind, target) for a branch or jump at `addr`, else (None, None).

    kind is 'uncond' when the delay slot's successor is the target alone, and
    'cond' when it is either the target or the fall-through.  `bal` and
    `bgez $0` are always taken and are classified 'uncond' for that reason.
    """
    op = w >> 26
    rs, rt = rs_of(w), rt_of(w)
    tgt = addr + 4 + simm(w) * 4

    if op in (0x02, 0x03):                          # j jal
        return ('uncond', ((addr + 4) & 0xF0000000) | ((w & 0x03FFFFFF) << 2))
    if op == 0x00 and (w & 0x3F) in (0x08, 0x09):   # jr jalr
        return ('uncond', None)
    if op == 0x04 and rs == 0 and rt == 0:          # b == beq $0,$0
        return ('uncond', tgt)
    if op == 0x01 and rt in (0x01, 0x11) and rs == 0:   # bgez/bal on $zero
        return ('uncond', tgt)
    if op in (0x04, 0x05, 0x06, 0x07):              # beq bne blez bgtz
        return ('cond', tgt)
    if op in (0x14, 0x15, 0x16, 0x17):              # branch-likely (MIPS-II)
        return ('cond', tgt)
    if op == 0x01 and rt in (0x00, 0x01, 0x10, 0x11,
                             0x02, 0x03, 0x12, 0x13):
        # bltz bgez bltzal bgezal, plus the four MIPS-II branch-likely forms
        # bltzl bgezl bltzall bgezall.  The second group was missing until
        # 2026-08-24: a load in one of their delay slots had its successor
        # resolved to the fall-through alone, which is one of the two legs.
        return ('cond', tgt)
    if op in (0x10, 0x11, 0x12) and rs == 0x08:
        # bc0f / bc0t / bc1f / bc1t / bc2f / bc2t.  reads() already modelled the
        # coprocessor branches as reading no general register; this function did
        # not know they were branches at all.
        return ('cond', tgt)
    return (None, None)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Just enough disassembly to print evidence beside a verdict
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

def text(w):
    """A readable rendering, or `.word 0x...` when this decoder is not sure.

    Deliberately partial.  Its job is to let a reader overturn a verdict, not
    to be a disassembler; anything it does not recognise says so.
    """
    op = w >> 26
    rs, rt, rd, sa, f = rs_of(w), rt_of(w), rd_of(w), sa_of(w), w & 0x3F
    R = REGS
    if w == 0:
        return 'nop'
    if op in LOADS:
        return '%-5s %s,%d(%s)' % (LOADS[op], R[rt], simm(w), R[rs])
    if op in (0x28, 0x29, 0x2A, 0x2B, 0x2E):
        return '%-5s %s,%d(%s)' % ({0x28: 'sb', 0x29: 'sh', 0x2A: 'swl',
                                    0x2B: 'sw', 0x2E: 'swr'}[op],
                                   R[rt], simm(w), R[rs])
    if op == 0x00:
        n = {0x00: 'sll', 0x02: 'srl', 0x03: 'sra'}.get(f)
        if n:
            return '%-5s %s,%s,%d' % (n, R[rd], R[rt], sa)
        n = {0x04: 'sllv', 0x06: 'srlv', 0x07: 'srav'}.get(f)
        if n:
            return '%-5s %s,%s,%s' % (n, R[rd], R[rt], R[rs])
        if f == 0x08:
            return 'jr    %s' % R[rs]
        if f == 0x09:
            return 'jalr  %s,%s' % (R[rd], R[rs])
        n = {0x10: 'mfhi', 0x12: 'mflo'}.get(f)
        if n:
            return '%-5s %s' % (n, R[rd])
        n = {0x11: 'mthi', 0x13: 'mtlo'}.get(f)
        if n:
            return '%-5s %s' % (n, R[rs])
        n = {0x18: 'mult', 0x19: 'multu', 0x1A: 'div', 0x1B: 'divu'}.get(f)
        if n:
            return '%-5s %s,%s' % (n, R[rs], R[rt])
        n = {0x20: 'add', 0x21: 'addu', 0x22: 'sub', 0x23: 'subu', 0x24: 'and',
             0x25: 'or', 0x26: 'xor', 0x27: 'nor', 0x2A: 'slt',
             0x2B: 'sltu'}.get(f)
        if n:
            return '%-5s %s,%s,%s' % (n, R[rd], R[rs], R[rt])
        n = {0x0A: 'movz', 0x0B: 'movn'}.get(f)
        if n:
            return '%-5s %s,%s,%s' % (n, R[rd], R[rs], R[rt])
        if f == 0x0F:
            return 'sync'
        return '.word 0x%08x' % w
    if op == 0x01:
        n = {0x00: 'bltz', 0x01: 'bgez', 0x10: 'bltzal', 0x11: 'bgezal'}.get(rt)
        if n:
            return '%-5s %s,%+d' % (n, R[rs], simm(w) * 4)
        return '.word 0x%08x' % w
    if op in (0x02, 0x03):
        return '%-5s 0x%07x' % (('j', 'jal')[op - 2], (w & 0x03FFFFFF) << 2)
    if op in (0x04, 0x05):
        return '%-5s %s,%s,%+d' % (('beq', 'bne')[op - 4], R[rs], R[rt],
                                   simm(w) * 4)
    if op in (0x06, 0x07):
        return '%-5s %s,%+d' % (('blez', 'bgtz')[op - 6], R[rs], simm(w) * 4)
    if 0x08 <= op <= 0x0E:
        n = {0x08: 'addi', 0x09: 'addiu', 0x0A: 'slti', 0x0B: 'sltiu',
             0x0C: 'andi', 0x0D: 'ori', 0x0E: 'xori'}[op]
        v = simm(w) if op in (0x08, 0x09, 0x0A, 0x0B) else (w & 0xFFFF)
        return '%-5s %s,%s,%s' % (n, R[rt], R[rs],
                                  ('0x%x' % v) if v >= 0 else str(v))
    if op == 0x0F:
        return 'lui   %s,0x%x' % (R[rt], w & 0xFFFF)
    if op == 0x10:
        if rs == 0x00:
            return 'mfc0  %s,$%d' % (R[rt], rd)
        if rs == 0x04:
            return 'mtc0  %s,$%d' % (R[rt], rd)
        return '.word 0x%08x  (COP0 rs=%d, unnamed by any source held here)' \
            % (w, rs)
    return '.word 0x%08x' % w


def ascii_of(w):
    b = struct.pack('>I', w)
    return ''.join(chr(c) if 0x20 <= c < 0x7F else '.' for c in bytearray(b))


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The scan
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

class Result(object):
    def __init__(self):
        self.words = 0
        self.loads = 0
        self.nop_after = 0
        self.violations = []      # (vaddr, foff, mnem, rt, saddr, sword, why, lw)
        self.unresolved = []      # (vaddr, foff, reason) -- successor NOT known
        self.notes = []           # (vaddr, foff, reason) -- successor known,
                                  # but this file cannot rule out a second one


def successors(sp, idx, i):
    """([(index, why)], [unresolved reason]) for what executes after sp.words[i].

    Everything here is asked of ONE span.  A predecessor or a successor that is
    not in this span is not in this file, and saying so is the whole promise the
    docstring makes -- "it reports unresolved rather than silently checking the
    wrong word".  Until 2026-08-24 the word list was flat and this function
    happily read across a section boundary.
    """
    out, unres, notes = [], [], []
    kind, tgt = (None, None)
    if i > 0:
        kind, tgt = control_flow(sp.words[i - 1][1], sp.words[i - 1][0])
    elif sp.head_unknown:
        # A NOTE and not an unresolved, and the difference is not cosmetic.
        # The successor IS known -- it is the next word, and it is checked.
        # What cannot be ruled out is a SECOND possible successor, if the
        # linker ends up placing a branch immediately before this span.  That
        # is a limit of scanning an unlinked object, which is why the gate is
        # pointed at the linked image; stating it is not the same as failing
        # on it, and failing on it would fail every .o whose first instruction
        # happens to be a load.
        notes.append('this load is word 0 of %s. Its successor is checked, but '
                     'nothing in this file says what executes BEFORE it -- if '
                     'the linker puts a branch there, this load is in a delay '
                     'slot and has a second successor. Gate on the linked image'
                     % sp.name)

    have_next = i + 1 < len(sp.words)

    if kind is None:
        if have_next:
            out.append((i + 1, 'next word'))
        else:
            unres.append('the load is the last word of %s; its successor is not '
                         'in this file' % sp.name)
        return out, unres, notes

    pa, pw = sp.words[i - 1][0], sp.words[i - 1][1]
    # A PC-relative displacement survives having the whole section placed
    # somewhere; an absolute `j`/`jal` target field does not, and in an
    # unlinked object it is a placeholder the linker has not written yet.
    placeholder = ((pw >> 26) in (0x02, 0x03)
                   and (sp.relocatable or not sp.addressed))

    if kind == 'uncond':
        if tgt is None:
            unres.append('the load is in the delay slot of a register jump at '
                         '0x%08x; the successor is not statically known' % pa)
        elif placeholder:
            unres.append('the load is in the delay slot of the jump at 0x%08x in '
                         'an unlinked object; its target field is a placeholder, '
                         'so the successor is not statically known' % pa)
        elif tgt in idx:
            out.append((idx[tgt], 'jump target of 0x%08x' % pa))
        else:
            unres.append('the load is in the delay slot of a jump at 0x%08x to '
                         '0x%08x, outside %s' % (pa, tgt, sp.name))
        return out, unres, notes

    # Conditional: either leg can be the next instruction, so both are checked.
    if placeholder:
        unres.append('the load is in the delay slot of the branch at 0x%08x in '
                     'an unlinked object; its taken leg is not statically known'
                     % pa)
    elif tgt in idx:
        out.append((idx[tgt], 'taken leg of 0x%08x' % pa))
    else:
        unres.append('the load is in the delay slot of the branch at 0x%08x '
                     'whose taken leg 0x%08x is outside %s' % (pa, tgt, sp.name))
    if have_next:
        out.append((i + 1, 'not-taken leg of 0x%08x' % pa))
    else:
        unres.append('the not-taken leg of the branch at 0x%08x is outside %s'
                     % (pa, sp.name))
    return out, unres, notes


def scan(spans):
    """spans is [Span], each already loaded.  Nothing crosses a span boundary."""
    r = Result()
    for sp in spans:
        r.words += len(sp.words)
        idx = {}
        for i, aw in enumerate(sp.words):
            idx[aw[0]] = i

        for i, (a, w) in enumerate(sp.words):
            ld = load_of(w)
            if ld is None:
                continue
            mnem, rt = ld
            r.loads += 1
            foff = sp.off + i * 4
            # "followed by an explicit nop" is a statement about the NEXT WORD,
            # and that is what the 646 in the population control counts.
            if i + 1 < len(sp.words) and sp.words[i + 1][1] == 0:
                r.nop_after += 1

            succ, unres, notes = successors(sp, idx, i)
            for reason in unres:
                r.unresolved.append((a, foff, reason))
            for reason in notes:
                r.notes.append((a, foff, reason))
            for j, why in succ:
                w2 = sp.words[j][1]
                # MIPS-I exempts the unaligned pair by name: lwl then lwr, or
                # the reverse, on the same rt is the defined idiom.
                #
                # **Only the rt merge is exempt.**  The successor's BASE
                # register is an ordinary read in the delay slot:
                # `lwl t0,0(t1); lwr t0,3(t0)` takes the register being loaded
                # as an address and gets the value from before the load.  Until
                # 2026-08-24 the exemption skipped the whole successor, so it
                # suppressed that -- and nothing else, because reads() did not
                # model the merge either.  Both halves are fixed together.
                if (w >> 26) in MERGING and (w2 >> 26) in MERGING \
                        and rt_of(w2) == rt and (w2 >> 26) != (w >> 26) \
                        and rs_of(w2) != rt:
                    continue
                if rt in reads(w2):
                    r.violations.append((a, foff, mnem, rt,
                                         sp.words[j][0], w2, why, w))
    return r


def survey(spans):
    """Hazard shapes this tool has NO rule for.  Counts, never verdicts.

    `C-9`/`F47` -- stores, `mflo`/`mfhi`, `mfc0`/`mtc0` -- is open and `R1b`
    owns it.  Printing a count is worth more than silence, because the shapes
    are what R1b goes and measures.  Calling any of them a violation would be a
    verdict without a rule.
    """
    out = {'mult/div then mfhi/mflo': [], 'mtc0 then mfc0': [],
           'a load sitting in a delay slot': []}
    for sp in spans:
      words = sp.words
      for i in range(len(words) - 1):
        a, w = words[i]
        n = words[i + 1][1]
        op, f = w >> 26, w & 0x3F
        nop_, nf = n >> 26, n & 0x3F
        if op == 0 and f in (0x18, 0x19, 0x1A, 0x1B) \
                and nop_ == 0 and nf in (0x10, 0x12):
            out['mult/div then mfhi/mflo'].append(a)
        if op == 0x10 and rs_of(w) == 0x04 and nop_ == 0x10 and rs_of(n) == 0x00:
            out['mtc0 then mfc0'].append(a)
        if load_of(n) and control_flow(w, a)[0] is not None:
            out['a load sitting in a delay slot'].append(words[i + 1][0])
    return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ISA-level conformance -- the fourth check, kept as a subsidiary
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The watch list is "what this core is not known to implement", which is two
# different things and both belong on it:
#
#   * the four MIPS-I unaligned load/stores, because a Lexra core is MIPS-I
#     MINUS those -- `C-7`/`F51` owns that question and it is still open;
#   * everything above MIPS-I.
#
# `strict` requires the word to decode to a VALID instruction: the fields an
# encoding fixes at zero must be zero.  `loose` looks only at opcode and funct.
# Both are offered and both counts are printed, because the gap between them is
# the whole lesson of this fixture.  `0x0000004b` in the loader's SPI chip
# table is `movn` to a loose reader and is not an instruction at all to a
# strict one.  **A count quoted without naming its classifier is not a
# measurement**, and that is a claim this tool can demonstrate rather than
# assert -- see K6c.

ISA_TRAPS = {0x30: 'tge', 0x31: 'tgeu', 0x32: 'tlt', 0x33: 'tltu',
             0x34: 'teq', 0x36: 'tne'}
ISA_OPS = {0x22: ('lwl', 'MIPS-I unaligned'), 0x26: ('lwr', 'MIPS-I unaligned'),
           0x2A: ('swl', 'MIPS-I unaligned'), 0x2E: ('swr', 'MIPS-I unaligned'),
           0x14: ('beql', 'MIPS-II'), 0x15: ('bnel', 'MIPS-II'),
           0x16: ('blezl', 'MIPS-II'), 0x17: ('bgtzl', 'MIPS-II'),
           0x2F: ('cache', 'MIPS-II'), 0x30: ('ll', 'MIPS-II'),
           0x38: ('sc', 'MIPS-II'), 0x33: ('pref', 'MIPS-IV'),
           0x13: ('COP1X', 'MIPS-IV'), 0x1C: ('SPECIAL2', 'MIPS32'),
           0x1F: ('SPECIAL3', 'MIPS32r2'), 0x1D: ('jalx', 'MIPS16'),
           0x11: ('COP1', 'FPU'), 0x31: ('lwc1', 'FPU'),
           0x35: ('ldc1', 'FPU'), 0x39: ('swc1', 'FPU'), 0x3D: ('sdc1', 'FPU')}
ISA_S2 = {0x00: 'madd', 0x01: 'maddu', 0x02: 'mul', 0x04: 'msub',
          0x05: 'msubu', 0x20: 'clz', 0x21: 'clo', 0x3F: 'sdbbp'}


def isa_hit(w, strict):
    """(mnemonic, level) if w is outside this core's known ISA, else None."""
    op = w >> 26
    rs, rt, rd, sa, f = rs_of(w), rt_of(w), rd_of(w), sa_of(w), w & 0x3F

    if op == 0x00:
        if w == 0:
            return None
        if f in (0x0A, 0x0B):
            if strict and sa != 0:
                return None
            return (('movz', 'movn')[f - 0x0A], 'MIPS-IV')
        if f == 0x0F:
            if strict and (rs or rt or rd):
                return None
            return ('sync', 'MIPS-II')
        if f in ISA_TRAPS:
            return (ISA_TRAPS[f], 'MIPS-II')
        return None

    if op == 0x1C:
        if not strict:
            return ('SPECIAL2/%s' % ISA_S2.get(f, '0x%02x' % f), 'MIPS32')
        if f in (0x00, 0x01, 0x04, 0x05) and rd == 0 and sa == 0:
            return (ISA_S2[f], 'MIPS32')
        if f == 0x02 and sa == 0:
            return ('mul', 'MIPS32')
        if f in (0x20, 0x21) and rt == rd and sa == 0:
            return (ISA_S2[f], 'MIPS32')
        if f == 0x3F:
            return ('sdbbp', 'MIPS32')
        return None

    if op == 0x1F:
        if not strict:
            return ('SPECIAL3/0x%02x' % f, 'MIPS32r2')
        if f in (0x00, 0x01, 0x02, 0x03, 0x04, 0x06, 0x07):
            return ('ext/ins', 'MIPS32r2')
        if f == 0x20 and rs == 0:
            return ('bshfl', 'MIPS32r2')
        if f == 0x3B and (w & 0x07C0) == 0:
            return ('rdhwr', 'MIPS32r2')
        return None

    if op == 0x11:
        if not strict:
            return ('COP1', 'FPU')
        if rs in (0x00, 0x02, 0x04, 0x06) and (w & 0x07FF) == 0:
            return ({0: 'mfc1', 2: 'cfc1', 4: 'mtc1', 6: 'ctc1'}[rs], 'FPU')
        if rs == 0x08 and rt in (0, 1, 2, 3):
            return ('bc1', 'FPU')
        if rs in (0x10, 0x11, 0x14) and f in (0x00, 0x01, 0x02, 0x03, 0x04,
                                              0x05, 0x06, 0x07, 0x0C, 0x0D,
                                              0x20, 0x21, 0x24, 0x30, 0x32,
                                              0x3C, 0x3E):
            return ('cop1.fmt', 'FPU')
        return None

    if op == 0x13:
        if not strict:
            return ('COP1X', 'MIPS-IV')
        if f in (0x00, 0x01, 0x08, 0x09, 0x20, 0x21, 0x28, 0x29, 0x2C, 0x2D,
                 0x2E, 0x2F, 0x30, 0x31, 0x38, 0x39):
            return ('COP1X', 'MIPS-IV')
        return None

    if op in (0x16, 0x17) and strict and rt != 0:
        return None

    if op in ISA_OPS:
        return ISA_OPS[op]
    return None


def isa_scan(words, strict):
    out = []
    for a, w in words:
        h = isa_hit(w, strict)
        if h:
            out.append((a, w, h[0], h[1]))
    return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Getting words out of a file
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

def die(msg):
    sys.stderr.write('hazlint: %s\n' % msg)
    sys.exit(3)


SYNTH_BASE = 0xFF000000     # for sections a relocatable object has not placed


class Span(object):
    """One contiguous run of instructions, and the fact that it IS contiguous.

    Two `PT_LOAD`s, or two sections of a `.o`, are not one run of instructions.
    Concatenating them into a flat word list makes the last word of one the
    predecessor of the first word of the next, and an invented predecessor is
    how the first word of a section ends up treated as somebody else's delay
    slot.  Everything in this file that asks "what came before" or "what comes
    next" asks it of a Span and never across two.

    `addressed` is False when the address is this tool's invention rather than
    the file's: in a relocatable object every section has `sh_addr == 0`, so a
    synthetic base keeps them from colliding -- and any branch target computed
    inside such a span is meaningless, which is reported rather than followed.

    `relocatable` marks a span whose `j`/`jal` target fields are placeholders
    the linker has not filled in yet.  A computed target there is a fiction.
    """

    def __init__(self, off, size, vaddr, name,
                 addressed=True, relocatable=False, head_unknown=False):
        self.off, self.size = off, size
        self.vaddr, self.name = vaddr, name
        self.addressed, self.relocatable = addressed, relocatable
        # True when something outside this file may execute immediately before
        # word 0 -- a section or segment of a larger image.  For a raw payload
        # word 0 is the entry point and nothing precedes it.
        self.head_unknown = head_unknown
        self.words = []

    def load(self, b):
        for k in range(0, self.size, 4):
            self.words.append((self.vaddr + k,
                               struct.unpack_from('>I', b, self.off + k)[0]))
        return self


def _check_extent(b, off, size, what):
    """A span that runs past EOF is an error, never a silently shorter span.

    `p_filesz`/`sh_size` larger than the bytes present hides whatever was in the
    missing part, and a scan that quietly covered less than it said it did is
    the failure this whole tool exists to make impossible.
    """
    if off > len(b) or off + size > len(b):
        die('%s claims file[0x%x .. 0x%x) but the file is 0x%x bytes. Refusing '
            'to scan a shorter range than the header describes -- a silently '
            'truncated scan reports 0 violations for the part it never read'
            % (what, off, off + size, len(b)))


def elf_ranges(b, want_section=None):
    """(spans, unscanned) for an ELF32 big-endian MIPS object.

    Program headers for an executable -- a stripped image still has to be
    loadable.  Section headers for a relocatable `.o`, which has no segments at
    all; that is the case `rlxprobe`'s build hands this tool.

    `unscanned` lists the allocated parts this tool did NOT look at, so that
    "code sitting in a segment that is not marked executable" is visible in the
    report instead of being silently outside the population.
    """
    if b[:4] != b'\x7fELF':
        return None
    cls, data = bytearray(b[4:6])
    if cls != 1 or data != 2:
        die('not a 32-bit big-endian ELF. This project is big-endian only, and '
            'a little-endian object here is a toolchain fault rather than a '
            'file to scan')
    e_type, e_machine = struct.unpack_from('>HH', b, 0x10)
    if e_machine != 8:
        die('ELF e_machine is %d, not 8 (EM_MIPS)' % e_machine)

    out, unscanned = [], []
    if e_type in (2, 3):                       # ET_EXEC, ET_DYN
        phoff, = struct.unpack_from('>I', b, 0x1C)
        phentsize, phnum = struct.unpack_from('>HH', b, 0x2A)
        for i in range(phnum):
            o = phoff + i * phentsize
            p_type, p_off, p_vaddr, _, p_filesz, _, p_flags, _ = \
                struct.unpack_from('>8I', b, o)
            if p_type != 1 or not p_filesz:    # PT_LOAD only
                continue
            if p_flags & 1:                    # PF_X
                _check_extent(b, p_off, p_filesz, 'PT_LOAD#%d' % i)
                out.append(Span(p_off, p_filesz, p_vaddr, 'PT_LOAD#%d' % i,
                                head_unknown=True))
            else:
                unscanned.append(('PT_LOAD#%d' % i, p_filesz,
                                  'not PF_X -- flags %d' % p_flags))
        if not out:
            die('no executable PT_LOAD segment in this ELF -- there is nothing '
                'to scan, and a tool that scanned nothing would report 0 '
                'violations')
        return out, unscanned

    # ET_REL and anything else: executable sections.
    shoff, = struct.unpack_from('>I', b, 0x20)
    shentsize, shnum, shstrndx = struct.unpack_from('>HHH', b, 0x2E)
    if shoff == 0 or shnum == 0:
        die('this ELF has neither an executable segment nor section headers')
    stroff, = struct.unpack_from('>I', b, shoff + shstrndx * shentsize + 0x10)
    rel = (e_type == 1)
    synth = 0
    for i in range(shnum):
        o = shoff + i * shentsize
        sh_name, sh_type, sh_flags, sh_addr, sh_off, sh_size = \
            struct.unpack_from('>6I', b, o)
        end = b.index(b'\0', stroff + sh_name)
        name = b[stroff + sh_name:end].decode('ascii', 'replace')
        exec_ = (sh_type == 1 and (sh_flags & 0x4))       # PROGBITS, EXECINSTR
        if want_section is not None:
            if name != want_section:
                continue
        elif not exec_:
            if (sh_flags & 0x2) and sh_size and sh_type == 1:   # SHF_ALLOC
                unscanned.append((name, sh_size, 'allocated but not EXECINSTR'))
            continue
        if not sh_size:
            continue
        _check_extent(b, sh_off, sh_size, 'section %r' % name)
        # In a relocatable object nothing has been placed, so every section
        # reports sh_addr == 0.  Left alone they all start at the same address,
        # the address->index map keeps only the last, and a branch target
        # resolves into a different section entirely.  A synthetic base per
        # section keeps them apart, and `addressed=False` is what stops any
        # target computed from it being believed.
        if sh_addr == 0 and rel:
            vaddr, addressed = SYNTH_BASE + synth, False
            synth += (sh_size + 0xFFF) & ~0xFFF
        else:
            vaddr, addressed = sh_addr, True
        out.append(Span(sh_off, sh_size, vaddr, name,
                        addressed=addressed, relocatable=rel,
                        head_unknown=True))
    if not out:
        die('no executable section in this ELF%s -- nothing to scan'
            % ('' if want_section is None else ' named %r' % want_section))
    return out, unscanned


def load_spans(b, spans):
    """Trim each span to a whole number of words and read them in."""
    live = []
    for sp in spans:
        sp.size -= sp.size % 4
        if sp.size <= 0:
            continue
        live.append(sp.load(b))
    return live


def raw_span(b, vma, lo=0, hi=None):
    """The `--raw` span.  Every caller goes through this, K4 included.

    K4 used to build its words with a private helper, so the population control
    exercised a code path no user file ever took and could not have caught a
    defect in span construction.  Found by the adversarial review 2026-08-24 and
    it was the sharpest of the seventeen, because it made the control look like
    it was guarding something it was not.
    """
    hi = len(b) if hi is None else hi
    return load_spans(b, [Span(lo, hi - lo, vma + lo, 'raw')])


